\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\title{Notes on Matrix Calculus for Estimation Problems}
\author{Study group notes}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Notation}
We write the design matrix as $A$ and its rows as observations.
The entry $A_i_j$ of the matrix is the value taken by
regressor $j$ on observation $i$.
Columns are collected into the vector $a_j$ when a single
regressor is discussed.

\section{The Normal Equations}
Minimising the residual sum of squares leads to the normal
equations $A^{T} A \hat{x} = A^{T} b$,
which have a unique solution when the columns of $A$ are
linearly independent.

\section{Remarks}
The same algebra covers weighted problems after a diagonal
rescaling of the rows of $A$.
\end{document}

